1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,208
How much solar can a distribution feeder accommodate?

2
00:00:04,208 --> 00:00:11,000
This project uses Python and a thirty-three-bus benchmark to investigate voltage limits.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:18,625
Before adding solar, the original feeder reached a minimum voltage of only ninety-one point three percent of nominal.

4
00:00:18,625 --> 00:00:26,042
The solar study uses a separate operating case, with source voltage set to one point zero three per unit.

5
00:00:26,042 --> 00:00:34,708
Four synthetic demand and solar profiles are evaluated at fifteen-minute intervals. These are not field measurements.

6
00:00:34,708 --> 00:00:43,750
Solar is placed across the feeder, near the supply, or at remote buses. Each comparison uses the same installed capacity.

7
00:00:43,750 --> 00:00:51,667
Volt-VAR control adjusts reactive power to support voltage, while respecting inverter capability limits.

8
00:00:51,667 --> 00:01:01,792
For remote solar, the largest tested voltage-feasible capacity rose from about 1.83 to 3.02 megawatts with control.

9
00:01:01,792 --> 00:01:11,625
At the same installed capacity, control reduced peak voltage, but increased total line-loss energy by about five point six percent.

10
00:01:11,625 --> 00:01:21,167
An independent radial solver was checked against pandapower at fifty-four operating points. Automated tests also check physical behavior.

11
00:01:21,167 --> 00:01:30,958
Sensitivity studies vary demand, source voltage, inverter size, and time resolution. The answer depends on these assumptions.

12
00:01:30,958 --> 00:01:41,208
Some high-capacity control calculations did not converge. These remain unresolved, and are not treated as physical violations.

13
00:01:41,208 --> 00:01:52,167
The repository includes code, tests, result tables, and an engineering report. These voltage-only findings are not utility connection approval.
